\begin{proof}
By Sylow's theorem, the number n_{19} of Sylow 19-subgroups divides 153 and is congruent to 1 modulo 19. Therefore n_{19}=1 or 153. If n_{19}=1, then the Sylow 19-subgroup is normal, and we are done.

Now suppose n_{19}=153. The number n_{17} of Sylow 17-subgroups divides 171 and is congruent to 1 modulo 17, so n_{17}=1 or 171. If n_{17}=1, then the Sylow 17-subgroup is normal, and we are done.

It remains to rule out n_{17}=171. Distinct subgroups of order 19 intersect trivially, so the 153 Sylow 19-subgroups contain 153\cdot18=2754 distinct nonidentity elements. Likewise, the 171 Sylow 17-subgroups contain 171\cdot16=2736 distinct nonidentity elements. A subgroup of order 17 cannot share a nonidentity element with a subgroup of order 19, since their intersection would have order dividing both 17 and 19. Hence these two collections are disjoint, yielding 2754+2736>2906 nonidentity elements, whereas G has only |G|-1=2906 nonidentity elements. This contradiction shows that n_{17}\ne171.

Thus one of the Sylow 19- or 17-subgroups is unique and normal. Therefore G has a nontrivial proper normal subgroup and is not simple.
\end{proof}
